findings.body:
`cloud/serviceLauncherClient.ts:232`

This module owns construction (`make` acquires `ServiceLauncherHostProcess` and `HostProcessEnvironment` and can fail), but it exports no canonical `layer`, so `server.ts` runs `make()` by hand and hand-rolls `Layer.succeed(ServiceLauncherClient.ServiceLauncherClient, launcherClient)`. Since `ServiceLauncherClient` is a `Context.Reference` with an unmanaged default, a consumer that misses that ad-hoc layer silently gets the fallback instead of a type error.

Consider exporting the canonical layer here and providing that single layer value in `server.ts` (layer memoization keeps one instance across the two `Layer.provide` sites):

```diff
 import * as Effect from "effect/Effect";
+import * as Layer from "effect/Layer";
 import * as Schema from "effect/Schema";
```

