🟠 **High** `Terminal/TerminalSurfaceView.swift:867`

When the software Ctrl modifier is armed and the next input is a multi-character string (e.g. pasting `abc` after tapping CTRL), `sendInput` emits only `Ctrl-A` and silently discards the remaining `bc`. `applyingControl(to:)` transforms only `unicodeScalars.first`, so `"abc"` becomes `\u{01}` and the rest is lost. Consider applying the control transform only to single-character input and passing the rest through unchanged.

```suggestion
    private func sendInput(_ data: String) {
        guard isRunning, !data.isEmpty else { return }
        let resolved: String
        if pendingModifier == .control {
            if data.count == 1 {
                resolved = TerminalHardwareKeyEncoder.applyingControl(to: data)
            } else {
                resolved = data
            }
        } else if pendingModifier == .command {
            resolved = "\u{1B}\(data)"
        } else {
            resolved = data
        }
        pendingModifier = nil
        onInput?(resolved)
    }
```

