`handleKeyDown` is bound to the outer `role="button"` container. Key events from the nested discard `<button>` bubble to that container. When a keyboard user focuses the discard button and presses Space, `handleKeyDown` calls `event.preventDefault()` and `onNavigate(draftId)`. `preventDefault()` on keydown suppresses the browser's synthesized click for Space, so `handleDiscard` never runs and the app navigates to the draft instead. With Enter the browser dispatches the click on keydown, so both `onDiscard` and `onNavigate` run.

Restrict the handler to key events that originate on the row itself.

<details>
<summary>♿ Proposed fix</summary>

```diff
   const handleKeyDown = useCallback(
     (event: ReactKeyboardEvent) => {
+      // Key events from the nested discard button bubble here; only the row
+      // itself activates navigation.
+      if (event.target !== event.currentTarget) {
+        return;
+      }
       if (event.key === "Enter" || event.key === " ") {
         event.preventDefault();
         onNavigate(draftId);
       }
     },
     [draftId, onNavigate],
   );
```
</details>

✅ Confirmed as addressed by @t3dotgg

🟡 **Medium** `components/Sidebar.tsx:586`

When `clearComposerContent` empties the active draft's prompt and attachments, the sidebar row for that draft does not disappear — it keeps rendering the frozen snapshot with stale content. The frozen-row branch at line 590 only checks that the live session is still non-promoted, but never re-checks `composerDraftHasUserContent` against the live composer state. So after content is cleared (e.g. the standalone slash-command path), `frozenActive.row` is still pushed even though the draft no longer has user content. Consider gating the frozen snapshot on the live `draftsByThreadKey[draftKey]` predicate before rendering.

